\subsection{Downstream Accuracy Validation}
\label{sec:accuracy-validation}

To validate that the PPL-based severity gradient translates to downstream task performance, we evaluate MATH-500 accuracy under compound sparsity (importance-based eviction, \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}${=}$8192; Table~\ref{tab:accuracy}). Qwen's minimal truncation ($\leq 4\%$) provides a clean PPL-accuracy bridge.
Qwen ($n{=}100$; truncation $\leq 4\%$) shows gradual decline from $46\%$ (dense) through $s{=}0.25$ ($\delta{=}-4$ to $-13$\,pp) with a sharp cliff at $s{=}0.30$ ($19\%$, $\delta{=}-27$\,pp). DeepSeek ($n{=}50$) degrades more steeply ($74\% \to 4\%$) with a dual-regime structure: at $s \leq 0.20$, degradation is primarily truncation-driven (non-truncated accuracy remains $68$--$71\%$ at $s{=}0.15$--$0.20$ vs.\ $88\%$ dense); at $s \geq 0.25$, both truncation and reasoning quality collapse simultaneously (Appendix~\ref{app:accuracy-details}).
The two models exhibit distinct failure modes reflecting training-paradigm dependence: DeepSeek failures are \emph{truncation-dominated} (truncation $16\% \to 76\%$), while Qwen failures are \emph{wrong-answer-dominated} (truncation $\leq 4\%$, reasoning quality degrades directly).

We provide a correlational mechanistic analysis in terms of importance discrimination collapse in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\section{Importance Discrimination Collapse under Compound Sparsity}
\label{sec:analysis}

The mechanisms discussed here are \emph{correlational diagnostics} that illuminate but do not fully explain the $15$--$17{\times}$ severity divergence between strategies. Both strategies exhibit near-identical Gini drops ($<10\%$ difference), yet severity diverges by $15$--$17{\times}$---an order-of-magnitude mismatch indicating that aggregate concentration collapse alone is insufficient. The critical differentiator is \emph{structural specificity} of importance-based pruning, which systematically removes high-attention-score heads (\S\ref{sec:discrimination-collapse-main}).

\subsection{Importance Discrimination Collapse}
\label{sec:discrimination-collapse-main}

Head pruning systematically reduces the discriminative power of cumulative importance scores, suggesting a mechanism for the phase-strategy interaction. The Gini coefficient decreases by $0.030$--$0.081$ (DeepSeek, $6$--$17\%$ relative to dense baseline) and $0.010$--$0.020$ (Qwen, $2$--$4\%$) after pruning, with consistent negative deltas in all 16 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics-full}). Random eviction shows near-identical Gini drops (e.g., DeepSeek $s{=}0.25$: $-0.065$ vs.\ $-0.060$), confirming that collapse is a consequence of head pruning \emph{per se}. Yet importance-based severity is $15$--$17{\times}$ larger---the amplification arises because importance-based eviction \emph{depends on} the very scores that pruning degrades.

This pattern---\emph{importance discrimination collapse}---reflects eviction degenerating into noise-sensitive selection as score distributions approach uniformity. The effect is phase-dependent: low-sparsity phases have broadly distributed attention where collapse has maximum impact; high-sparsity phases preserve concentration. \cite{hies-2025} report this collapse under single-axis pruning; we extend it to the compound setting.

To provide quantitative evidence for structural specificity, we analyzed the attention Gini coefficients of importance-pruned versus retained heads. Importance pruning (Frobenius norm criterion) systematically removes heads with lower Gini coefficients---i.e., heads with more dispersed attention patterns (Mann-Whitney $U$, $p < 10^{-28}$; rank-biserial $r = 0.62$--$0.76$ across all 8 conditions). Random pruning, by contrast, samples heads with Gini indistinguishable from the population mean (200-trial verification). The Frobenius norm and Gini share sensitivity to attention magnitude; the large effect size ($r > 0.6$) nonetheless indicates strong structural separation.

We note that this evidence is correlational; causal validation through controlled manipulation of importance-score entropy is an important direction for future work.
%% TODO: Add per-phase Gini results when available from Agent computation.

\subsection{Entropy Redistribution: A Model-Dependent Secondary Effect}
\label{sec:entropy-redistribution-main}

As a supplementary observation, head pruning also redistributes the attention entropy of surviving heads on DeepSeek ($\Delta\mathcal{H} \in [-0.047, -0.040]$; Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:entropy-deltas}). However, entropy redistribution shows model-dependent behavior---the direction reverses on Qwen at $s{=}0.25$ ($\Delta\mathcal{H} = +0.045$)---replicating in only 2/4 Qwen conditions and thus only partially consistent. Importance discrimination collapse remains the more robust, model-independent correlate, though it partially accounts for rather than fully explains the severity divergence.

\section{Importance Discrimination Collapse Details}
\label{app:discrimination-collapse-details}

This cross-model consistency (Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics-full}) establishes importance discrimination collapse as a robust consequence of compound sparsity. When importance scores lose discriminative power, thresholding the bottom-$s$ fraction becomes noise-sensitive: small perturbations change which positions cross the eviction boundary. In the limit of perfectly uniform scores, importance-based eviction degenerates into an arbitrary selection functionally equivalent to biased random eviction. The same pattern holds under random eviction (DeepSeek: $-0.031$ to $-0.071$; Qwen: $-0.019$ to $-0.046$), confirming that importance discrimination collapse is a consequence of head pruning \emph{per se}, not of the eviction strategy.


\section{Extended Discussion}
\label{app:extended-discussion}

\paragraph{On the predictability of phase-modulated severity.}
The compound interaction is not uniformly fragile---it is selectively fragile in a phase-dependent manner ($\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\text{high}} < 0.06$ at $s \leq 0.30$).

\paragraph{Random masking as falsification control.}
At $n{=}500$, random eviction develops a detectable but weak phase gradient above $s \approx 0.20$: non-significant at $s \leq 0.20$ for both models, but significant at $s \geq 0.25$ (DS: $\Delta{=}0.26$--$0.51$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.006$; QW: $\Delta{=}0.39$--$0.91$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$). Where significant, the random phase gradient is $2.7$--$17{\times}$ weaker than importance-based (restricted to conditions where both strategies are significant, $s \geq 0.25$), confirming that the dominant amplification is strategy-specific.

\paragraph{Entropy mechanism boundary conditions.}
Directly measuring per-head entropy shifts across architecturally distinct models---and identifying what training-time properties determine whether entropy concentrates or disperses under head pruning---would clarify the boundary conditions of the full mechanism.


\section{Pilot Mitigation Experiment}
\label{app:pilot-mitigation}

We explored oracle phase-aware budget reallocation, shifting sparsity budget from high-severity low-sparsity phases to low-severity high-sparsity phases. At $s{=}0.25$, oracle recovery---the fraction of compound interaction eliminated by phase-aware allocation---reached 18\% (Wilcoxon $p = 0.002$), below the pre-registered 30\% gate for claiming practical mitigation. A heuristic variant achieved 32\% recovery ($p = 0.024$). \textbf{Critically, 95\% bootstrap CIs for both the oracle and heuristic conditions span zero}, meaning neither recovery estimate is statistically distinguishable from no improvement. These results indicate that phase-aware budget reallocation alone is insufficient to neutralize the severity gradient; effective mitigation likely requires joint optimization of budget allocation and eviction strategy selection across phases.


\section{Extended Limitations}
\label{app:limitations}

\paragraph{Model scale.}
All experiments are conducted at the 7B parameter scale.
Whether the phase-modulated severity patterns persist, amplify, or attenuate at larger scales (e.g., 70B) remains untested.
Larger models may exhibit different attention redundancy structures that alter the importance discrimination collapse dynamics (and the model-dependent entropy component may behave differently at scale).


\paragraph{Architectural scope.}
Our two models---DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct---share the Qwen2ForCausalLM architecture.
The cross-training-paradigm replication (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}) establishes that the findings are not artifacts of a single model's weights, though both models share the same Qwen2ForCausalLM architecture.
Preliminary cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, \S\ref{sec:cross-arch}, Appendix~\ref{app:cross-arch}) shows qualitatively consistent within-importance phase gradients ($d_z \sim 1.0$) but a substantially weaker strategy $\times$ phase interaction than in Qwen2-based architectures; random pruning also exhibits a non-trivial phase gradient on LLaMA, reducing the strategy contrast. This partial confirmation ($s{=}0.30$ Wilcoxon $p_{\text{Bonf}}{=}0.029$; $s \leq 0.25$ non-significant) suggests the qualitative pattern may generalize but quantitative impact is architecture-dependent. Comprehensive cross-architecture validation across more diverse designs (e.g., Mistral, multi-latent attention architectures) and scales remains an important next step.


\paragraph{Task coverage.}
We evaluate exclusively on MATH-500, a mathematical reasoning benchmark.
Different reasoning tasks---code generation, commonsense reasoning, multi-hop question answering---may induce different phase structures with different sparsity distributions, potentially altering the severity gradient profile.
Generalizing the phase-strategy interaction to broader reasoning domains is an important next step.


\paragraph{Statistical scope.}
Bootstrap confidence intervals use $B{=}10{,}000$ resamples (seed$=$42). The primary analysis uses $n{=}496$ paired problems for DeepSeek (4 excluded) and $n{=}475$ for Qwen (25 excluded); the permutation test (Table~\ref{tab:permutation}) uses $n{=}49$ from an initial profiling subset.
The severity difference distribution is right-skewed (mean/median ratios up to $43{:}1$), motivating our use of medians and nonparametric tests rather than parametric alternatives.
All four profiling-subset permutation tests reject $H_0$ after Bonferroni correction ($p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$; Table~\ref{tab:permutation}), with CI lower bounds increasing from $0.19$ to $6.70$---confirming the effect direction at every sparsity level. At $n{=}496$, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests confirm all conditions with $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-13}$ (Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d}). The permutation CIs at higher sparsity are wider (e.g., $[6.70,\; 16.29]$ at $s{=}0.30$), reflecting genuine variance in per-problem severity amplification.

At $n{=}500$, random eviction on both models becomes significant at $s \geq 0.25$ (Qwen: $\Delta{=}0.39$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$ at $s{=}0.25$; $\Delta{=}0.91$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-13}$ at $s{=}0.30$; bootstrap CIs exclude zero).
These effects are genuine but $2.7$--$20{\times}$ weaker than importance-based eviction at the same sparsity levels, consistent with a weak phase gradient that is detectable at sufficient sample size but not the primary driver of compound severity.

\paragraph{Sparsity range.}
Our primary analysis spans $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$; pilot data at $s{=}0.10$ and $s{=}0.35$ exist (6 total sparsity levels) but are not included in the main analysis due to limited sample size at these boundary conditions. Monotonicity is established at the tested sparsity levels (JT test $p \ll 10^{-10}$); the functional form of $G(s)$ between and beyond these levels is not characterized.
More extreme sparsity ($s > 0.50$) may exhibit qualitatively different interaction dynamics---the sub-exponential growth pattern suggests the severity gradient may plateau, but this remains unverified.


\paragraph{No throughput evaluation.}
We do not report inference throughput or latency, which are necessary for deployment analysis but orthogonal to our severity characterization.



